From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 19 Aug 2026 11:20:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 39/39] vc4gfx: word 0 is a display list, not the absence of one

With the registers read in the right byte order (patch 0038) the Pi 3
dump is finally readable, and it says the takeover was right about the
channel and wrong about the list:

    [VC4HVS] ID=0x64647276 DISPCTRL=0x9a0c0fff
    [VC4HVS] ch1: LIST=0000 LACT=0000 CTRL=80780438 ...
    [VC4HVS] PV2: hactive=1920 hfp=88 hsync=44 hbp=148
                  vactive=1080 vfp=4 vsync=5 vbp=36
    [VC4HVS] takeover: HDMI channel not usable (CTRL=80780438 head=0)

CTRL now decodes as ENABLE | 1920 << 12 | 1080, PV2 carries real 1080p
timings, and the firmware's PPF kernel reads back as a symmetric 11-tap
filter. Everything is where it should be. LACT really is zero -- and zero
is where the firmware's boot display list starts.

arch/arm-native never meets this. By the time vc4gfx runs there it has
already asked the firmware for a framebuffer, and that FBALLOC makes the
firmware rebuild its list in the middle of dlist RAM -- word 0x664 on the
Pi 3 that produced the reference dumps, which is exactly what the second
dump in yesterday's log showed once a mode had been set. This port asks
for nothing: Emu68 sets the mode up before AROS starts, so what is on the
HDMI channel is still the boot list, at the bottom of dlist RAM. Linux's
vc4 says the same from the other side: it reserves the first words as
"the bootloader's dlist" and allocates above them rather than treating
word 0 as free.

So the head being in range is all this check can honestly test. Whether
the list leads anywhere was always decided further down, by walking it
and finding a plane whose PTR0 is our framebuffer; a head of zero that
leads nowhere still declines there, one entry later. The dump gets the
same treatment, and now walks any channel the HVS has enabled.
--- a/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_hvs.c
+++ b/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_hvs.c
@@ -219,7 +219,7 @@
     for (attempt = 0; attempt < 3 && !stable; attempt++)
     {
         head = hvs_rd(HVS_DISPLACT(HVS_CHANNEL_HDMI));
-        if (head == 0 || head >= HVS_DLIST_WORDS)
+        if (head >= HVS_DLIST_WORDS)
         {
             bug("[VC4HVS] probe: bad list head %u, aborting\n", head);
             return;
@@ -679,8 +679,27 @@
 
         ctrl = hvs_rd(HVS_DISPCTRLX(HVS_CHANNEL_HDMI));
         head = hvs_rd(HVS_DISPLACT(HVS_CHANNEL_HDMI));
-        if (!(ctrl & HVS_DISPCTRLX_ENABLE) || head == 0
-            || head >= HVS_DLIST_WORDS)
+
+        /*
+         * head == 0 is a list, not the absence of one.
+         *
+         * arch/arm-native never sees it because by the time vc4gfx runs
+         * there it has already asked the firmware for a framebuffer, and
+         * that FBALLOC makes the firmware rebuild its list somewhere in
+         * the middle of dlist RAM (word 0x664, on the Pi 3 that produced
+         * the reference dumps). This port asks for nothing: Emu68 sets
+         * the mode up before AROS starts, so what is still on the HDMI
+         * channel is the firmware's *boot* list, and that one sits at the
+         * bottom of dlist RAM. Linux's vc4 says the same thing from the
+         * other side -- it reserves the first words as "the bootloader's
+         * dlist" and allocates above them rather than describing word 0
+         * as free.
+         *
+         * The head being in range is all this can honestly check. Whether
+         * the list leads anywhere is decided below, by walking it and
+         * finding a plane that points at our framebuffer.
+         */
+        if (!(ctrl & HVS_DISPCTRLX_ENABLE) || head >= HVS_DLIST_WORDS)
         {
             bug("[VC4HVS] takeover: HDMI channel not usable "
                 "(CTRL=%08x head=%u)\n", ctrl, head);
@@ -1098,9 +1117,14 @@
             hvs_rd(HVS_DISPCTRLX(ch)), hvs_rd(HVS_DISPBKGNDX(ch)),
             hvs_rd(HVS_DISPSTATX(ch)), hvs_rd(HVS_DISPBASEX(ch)));
 
-        /* An idle channel parks LIST/LACT at 0 or at an END word; only
-         * walk lists that lead anywhere. */
-        if (lact != 0 && lact < HVS_DLIST_WORDS)
+        /* Word 0 is a real list head, not an idle marker: a firmware
+         * boot display list starts at the bottom of dlist RAM, which is
+         * why Linux's vc4 reserves the first words as "the bootloader's
+         * dlist" and allocates above them. Walk any channel the HVS has
+         * enabled and let the entry decode say whether it leads
+         * anywhere. */
+        if ((hvs_rd(HVS_DISPCTRLX(ch)) & HVS_DISPCTRLX_ENABLE)
+            && lact < HVS_DLIST_WORDS)
         {
             bug("[VC4HVS]  walking active list of ch%d:\n", ch);
             hvs_dump_list(lact, fb_phys, fb_pitch, fb_width, fb_height);
